<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Julius von Pflug</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1499">1499</a>–<a href="/years/1564">1564</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Julius von Pflug was a German humanist and Catholic theologian who sought reconciliation with the Lutherans and helped draft the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> Interim of 1548. Elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg">Naumburg</a> in 1541, he was kept out by the Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-of-amsdorf">Nicholas of Amsdorf</a> until 1547. As the last Catholic bishop of Naumburg, his death in 1564 left the see to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> administrators.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/julius-von-pflug.jpg" alt="Julius von Pflug" width="320" height="416" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Julius_von_Pflug_(detail).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Julius von Pflug was born at Eythra in 1499, the son of Cæsar von Pflug, who served as commissary for the Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> at the Leipzig Disputation of 1519. He studied at Leipzig from 1510 to 1517 and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> from 1517 to 1519, became a canon in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meissen">Meissen</a> and went back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, to Bologna and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/padua">Padua</a>, before Duke George drew him home in 1521 with offers of preferment. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dresden">Dresden</a> and Leipzig he gave himself chiefly to humanist studies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1530 he went with Duke George to the Diet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> and began writing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a>, setting out a plan to restore religious peace through moderate men such as Erasmus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Melanchthon</a>; Erasmus replied that matters had gone too far even for a council. As dean of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeitz">Zeitz</a> from 1532 he favoured lay communion under both kinds, the marriage of priests and moral reform, and he took part in the Leipzig Colloquy of 1534 and the Regensburg Conference of 1541, where he disputed with Johannes Eck and Johann Gropper.</p><p>Named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naumburg">Naumburg</a> in 1541, he accepted his election on 15 January 1542, but the elector John Frederick of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> had Nicolaus von Amsdorf ordained by Luther in his place. At the Diet of Worms in 1545 the emperor ordered the elector to admit Pflug, and the matter was settled only by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a>. These struggles turned him into a bitter opponent of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a>. In his diocese, however, he left the Lutherans undisturbed, restored Catholic worship only in the chief church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeitz">Zeitz</a> and the cathedral of Naumburg, and for want of Catholic clergy kept the married ministers Amsdorf had installed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>The Augsburg Interim rested on his Formula sacrorum emendandorum, revised by Pflug with Michael Helding, Johannes Agricola, Domingo de Soto and Pedro de Malvenda. He also took part in the talks that produced the first draft of the Leipzig Interim, corresponding secretly with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-melanchthon">Melanchthon</a>. He attended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-trent">Council of Trent</a> briefly in November 1551, presided at the Colloquy of Worms in 1557, which the Flacians wrecked, and in 1562 published his Oratio de ordinanda republica Germaniœ. He died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeitz">Zeitz</a> on 3 September 1564, still hoping that the council would restore religious peace.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Julius von Pflug” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the last Catholic bishop, <b>Julius von Pflug</b>, died in 1564, the see came under Saxon administrators.</blockquote><a href="/state/prince-bishopric-of-naumburg">Prince-Bishopric of Naumburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Eythra, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeitz">Zeitz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.23,12.3&amp;year=1541" aria-label="Eythra on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4829 389 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Julius von Pflug"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5279 659h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5279 659h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.23,12.3&amp;year=1541">What was here in 1541? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
